course: dictionary
lesson: 9428
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Stable can mean steady and firm, or it can mean a building where horses are kept. The context, meaning the words around it, tells you which meaning fits.
explain: «Tall things stay up when they are stable: a wide base and most of the weight low down make them hard to tip over.» Here stable means steady and firm. The clues are wide base and hard to tip over, so the tower is steady, not likely to fall.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence in place of the hard word. «Tall things stay up when they are steady» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. In the Christmas carol, «Saw the star o'er a stable low» means a building where animals are kept, because the story is set with animals and a low building.
fact: Stable means steady and firm, not likely to move, wobble, fall or change.
fact: A stable is a building where horses are kept and fed.
fact: A stable person or situation is calm and not changing much, as in a patient in «stable condition».
fact: A stable structure has a wide base and does not tip over, so pupils build stable towers.
fact: «Riding stables» are places where you can ride horses, and «stable base» means a firm, steady bottom.
exercise: What does stable mean in «Tall things stay up when they are stable»? | *Steady and firm, not likely to tip over | Very tall and thin | Wobbly and likely to fall | Covered in snow
exercise: In the carol, «Saw the star o'er a stable low», what is a stable? | *A building where animals, such as horses, are kept and fed | A tall church tower with bells | A kind of star | A place for travellers to sleep in a town
exercise: A patient is in «stable condition». What does that tell you? | *The patient is calm and not changing much | The patient is running a race | The patient is sleeping in a stable | The patient is getting worse very quickly
test: A patient is in a «stable condition». What does that mean? | *Not getting worse, calm and not changing much | Getting worse every hour | Ready to run a race | Asleep in a building for horses
test: Which phrase uses stable to mean steady and firm? | Horses fed in the stable every morning | The stable carol singers | *A stable base | Riding stables
exam: What does «stables» mean in «there are stables and a very old banqueting-hall»? | Very old places | A dining hall for feasts | *Buildings where horses are kept | Steady and firm
